#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""SpaceQuilt - disk usage as a SpaceMonger-style nested map or icicle columns.

SpaceQuilt runs `du -k --all`, the way xdiskusage does, and draws the tree in
two views. Boxes: each directory is a titled box with its children packed
inside. Icicle: one column per depth, blocks sized by bytes. Double click a
block to drill into it; the breadcrumb or Backspace takes you back up.

Usage:
    spacequilt [PATH] [-x | -X] [--view VIEW] [--colors SCHEME] [--style STYLE]

  PATH               Directory to scan. Without it, a page lists the mounted
                     volumes and your recent scans (Ctrl+O opens it too).
  -x                 Stay on one filesystem (du -x), the default. A scan of /
                     then measures the root volume without the other disks,
                     /proc or /sys, and shows its free space as a block.
  -X                 Descend into every filesystem mounted below PATH.
  --view VIEW        boxes (default) or icicle. Ctrl+1 / Ctrl+2 switch live.
  --colors SCHEME    levels (one colour per depth), branches (one hue per
                     top-level entry, lighter with depth) or rainbow (each
                     level spreads the hues again). Sets the starting view's
                     scheme; boxes defaults to levels, icicle to branches.
  --style STYLE      flat, raised (default) or deep: how much relief the
                     blocks get.

SpaceQuilt remembers the view, the schemes and the style between runs.

Interaction:
  Wheel                  Zoom around the cursor.
  Shift / Ctrl + wheel   Icicle: zoom sizes / depth only.
  Drag                   Pan.
  Click                  Select a block.
  Double click           Drill in (on the focused block: go up).
  Backspace, Esc         Go up one level. Esc during a scan cancels it.
  0, Ctrl+0              Fit the focused directory to the window.
  Right click            Open in file manager, copy path.
  Ctrl+O                 Volumes, recent scans, browse.
  Ctrl+R                 Rescan.
  Ctrl+M                 One filesystem on or off.
  Ctrl+1 / Ctrl+2        Icicle / boxes view.
"""

